  "subtitle": "the machine that taught a computer to feel like a drum kit",
  "why_scout_cares": "The first programmable drum machine to use digital samples of real drums, and the first to introduce the pressure-sensitive pad grid as a musical programming interface. Twelve hard plastic pads respond to the player's strike velocity; the machine quantizes timing to sixteenth notes with an adjustable 'shuffle' (swing) delay. This is not just a drum machine — it is the ancestral interaction model for every pad controller, every MPC, and every finger-drumming interface that followed. The LM-1 made the drum machine a credible instrument rather than a toy, and the 4x4 pad grid it pioneered directly led to the Akai MPC, which transformed hip-hop production. Only 525 units were built at up to $5,500 each, making this the definition of 'expensive experiment that changed everything.' PD image on Wikimedia Commons.",
